WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.360
Today we are going to look at a new&nbsp;
AI agent that learned to play video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.520
games really well and learned some…&nbsp;
really interesting things. I’ll leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.340
it at that for now and show it to you in a moment.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:24.060
Now, first things first, the goal was to create&nbsp;
a large number of procedurally generated levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:30.600
these are created by a computer if you will.
Here you see their AI driving on the Hungarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:37.140
Formula 1 track. The goal is that it would start&nbsp;
to play increasingly difficult levels and see if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:43.740
it has proper zero-shot performance, that is,&nbsp;
can it play a level that it hasn’t seen yet. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:51.840
let’s switch to a previously unseen track. Great&nbsp;
job, little AI! But, why is this so interesting?

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.680
It is interesting because of two things. One,&nbsp;
it learned some really interesting skills in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.680
laser tag, a little shooter game. Look.&nbsp;
It learned to hide behind walls to evade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:11.160
these shots. So what happens if a fight&nbsp;
breaks out? Well, not a problem. It can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:18.060
also dodge these bullets. Or it gets better,&nbsp;
it can even run away from them. Loving it.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:25.920
And here comes the interesting part. Second,&nbsp;
this little AI learned to be a criminal. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:32.100
when you are asking, Doctor, how? Let me&nbsp;
show you these little shenanigans. It can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.340
not only drive on new levels, but it has also&nbsp;
learned to force an opponent off of the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:47.400
Ouch. It can also overtake by cutting corners.&nbsp;
Very creative. But the creative elimination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.540
the opponent does not stop there…oh my. Are you&nbsp;
seeing what I am seeing? Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:01.800
that is a hit and run. I can almost hear it say&nbsp;
“Bye-bye, see you next lap!”. And if you happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.820
to meet it again, this little criminal can also&nbsp;
block your cornering. And all this was trained on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:15.300
a single graphics card. Not a cheap one, but one&nbsp;
graphics card nonetheless. Really cool experiment.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:23.160
So what is all this good for? Well, this&nbsp;
technique could be extended to games that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:30.060
require more than 2 players and we could see what&nbsp;
kind of collaboration these AI agents can learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.780
together. Or… if you ask me, perhaps what kind of&nbsp;
criminal rings they create. Kidding, of course.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.780
And if you allow me, I would like to show you an&nbsp;
incredible computer graphics simulation paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:49.440
This one addresses something that we call the&nbsp;
volumetric dissipation problem. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.560
Well, look. We start the simulation with a&nbsp;
given number of particles, and over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:02.400
due to the inaccuracies of our calculations,&nbsp;
they slowly disappear. Like a magic trick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.180
Don’t believe it? Well, look. We started with&nbsp;
this many particles, and now we only have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:15.780
many. This new technique is able to create these&nbsp;
magical simulations where the amount of volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:23.520
over time remains the same. So, does it also&nbsp;
apply to messier scenes than this? You bet your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.900
papers it does! Check this out! The scene can&nbsp;
be an absolute mess, and it still works. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:38.340
it not only works, but it can easily plugged into&nbsp;
already existing systems, and it will work up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:45.960
2.5 times faster than previous methods. And&nbsp;
all this with volume preservation. Loving it.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:53.760
Now, one more interesting thing. Look. Only&nbsp;
500 people have seen this paper yet. So I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.140
really worried that if we don’t talk about&nbsp;
these incredible computer graphics research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:03.720
works here on Two Minute Papers, perhaps no&nbsp;
one will talk about them. If you liked this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:47.760
please share it with your friends. Let’s keep&nbsp;
the spark alive and show these amazing graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.260
papers to more new Fellow Scholars.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.340
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

